<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The BPTT Problem</div>
<h2>Why training RNNs is so hard</h2>

<p>Recurrent neural networks hold a compelling promise: a fixed-size memory that summarizes the entire past, updated one token at a time. At inference, they require <em>O(1)</em> compute and memory per token — unlike Transformers that must attend to every previous token. But this promise has been largely unfulfilled, because training RNNs is broken at the foundation.</p>

<p>The standard algorithm — Backpropagation Through Time (BPTT) — unrolls the RNN across the full sequence and propagates gradients backward. For a sequence of length T, the gradient of the loss with respect to memory state m<sub>t</sub> must travel through up to T nonlinear transition functions. This creates two notorious failure modes:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Vanishing gradients:</strong> If the transition Jacobian has singular values &lt; 1, gradients decay exponentially. The RNN develops a recency bias — it only remembers the last few tokens.</li>
  <li><strong>Exploding gradients:</strong> If singular values &gt; 1, gradients blow up. The induced dynamical system becomes chaotic and training diverges.</li>
</ul>

<p>The visualization below shows the gradient magnitude ∂L/∂m<sub>t</sub> at each timestep for BPTT vs. SMT in a needle retrieval task. The loss is applied only at the <em>last</em> timestep (the model must report what it saw at timestep 1). Drag the slider to see how different random initializations affect BPTT gradients — but have essentially no effect on SMT.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">gradient_stability.py — Gradient magnitude ∂L/∂m_t vs. timestep</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag slider — init seed</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="grad-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
