</div>

<p>BPTT is also <strong>sequential in time</strong>: you cannot compute m<sub>t+1</sub> without first computing m<sub>t</sub>, which requires m<sub>t-1</sub>, and so on. Modern hardware is massively parallel, but BPTT cannot use it. The training throughput scales only with parallelism over the <em>batch</em> dimension, not the sequence.</p>

<p>Linear RNNs (Mamba, RWKV, GLA) sidestep this with parallel scan algorithms — but only because their transition is <em>linear</em>. That linearity fundamentally limits what they can represent: they provably cannot track a chess board or perform state counting beyond a certain complexity. The question is whether we can train <em>nonlinear</em> RNNs in parallel.</p>
</section>


