<section id="s-background" data-code="background">
<div class="section-no">02 — Predictive States</div>
<h2>The key insight: memory is a prediction problem</h2>

<p>What should an RNN remember? The answer from information theory is clean: the memory m<sub>t</sub> should be a <em>sufficient statistic</em> of the past for predicting the future. This is called a <strong>predictive state</strong> — it retains everything relevant to the future and deliberately discards everything else.</p>

<p>In a story about two characters, the predictive state remembers their names and personalities — information needed to predict future dialogue — but discards irrelevant details like what they wore on page 3. The future-prediction criterion automatically selects what to compress.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Predictive State Representation</div>
  <span class="math-var">m<sub>t</sub></span> = <span class="math-var">Q</span>([<span class="math-var">x</span><sub>0</sub>, <span class="math-var">x</span><sub>1</sub>, ..., <span class="math-var">x</span><sub>t</sub>]) &nbsp; <span class="math-comment">— sufficient statistic of the past</span><br>
  Maximize <span class="math-var">p</span>(<span class="math-var">y</span><sub>t..T</sub> | <span class="math-var">m<sub>t</sub></span>) &nbsp; <span class="math-comment">— retain only future-relevant information</span><br>
  <br>
  <span class="math-var">m</span>* = argmin compression s.t. <span class="math-var">m</span> predicts <span class="math-var">y</span><sub>future</sub> &nbsp; <span class="math-comment">— the "ideal memory"</span>
</div>

<p>The crucial insight is that computing this predictive state does <em>not</em> need to be sequential. Yes, the past is a sequence — but we can equivalently represent it as a <strong>set of timestamped events</strong> &#123;(x0, 0), (x1, 1), ..., (xt, t)&#125;. A function over a set is permutation-invariant — and Transformers can compute permutation-invariant functions over sets <em>in parallel</em>.</p>

<p>This reframing is the foundation of SMT: use a bidirectional Transformer to compute ideal memory labels (what the memory <em>should</em> be), then train the RNN as a supervised regression problem — learn to predict the next memory state given the current one and the next input.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">predictive_state.py — What the memory should compress vs. discard</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click tokens to see what's kept</span>
  </div>
