<section id="s-memory" data-code="memory">
<div class="section-no">04 — Memory Space</div>
<h2>What the memory learns to look like</h2>

<p>To understand what SMT is actually learning, the authors trained models with a 2D memory state — so you can directly visualize every memory as a point in the plane. The results reveal something beautiful: the memory geometry adapts to what the future requires.</p>

<p>For a <strong>retrieval</strong> task (remember a "needle" value and report it at the end), SMT collapses many different past sequences into just a few effective memory states: an initial state, a "needle is coming" state, and one state per possible needle value. This is exactly a finite-state machine — and it's what the task requires.</p>

<p>For a <strong>string copy</strong> task (reproduce the entire past sequence), lossless compression is needed. You cannot alias distinct strings to the same memory. Instead, SMT learns a tree-like geometry where each branch encodes a different prefix of the string.</p>

<p>Choose a task below to see the learned memory geometry. The dots are encoder memories mt at different timesteps; arrows show the RNN's transition field (given "next input = 2", where does each memory move?).</p>

