<section id="s-drift" data-code="drift">
<div class="section-no">05 — Drift &amp; DMT</div>
<h2>The train-test mismatch problem, and how to fix it</h2>

<p>After SMT, there's a subtle problem. During training, the RNN learns to predict the next memory state given the <em>encoder's</em> memory as input: (mt, xt₊1) → mt₊1. But at test time, the encoder is gone — the RNN is unrolled with its <em>own</em> predicted memories as input: (m̂t, xt₊1) → m̂t₊1.</p>

<p>Even small one-step prediction errors compound over time. The RNN trajectory and the encoder trajectory diverge — a phenomenon called <strong>drift</strong>. This is the classic distribution shift problem in imitation learning: behavior cloning trains on expert observations, but at test time the agent sees its own (imperfect) observations.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">drift_correction.py — SMT vs DMT trajectory alignment</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">watch trajectories over time</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-bottom:.75rem">
      <button id="drift-play" style="padding:.3rem .8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--purple);background:var(--purple-dim);color:var(--purple-l)">▶ Play</button>
      <button id="drift-reset" style="padding:.3rem .8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">↺ Reset</button>
      <span id="drift-t" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);align-self:center;margin-left:.25rem">t = 0</span>
    </div>
    <canvas id="drift-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div class="canvas-legend" style="margin-top:.5rem">
      <div class="legend-item"><div class="legend-dot" style="background:#06b6d4"></div>Encoder trajectory (target)</div>
      <div class="legend-item"><div class="legend-dot" style="background:#ef4444"></div>SMT RNN (drifts away)</div>
      <div class="legend-item"><div class="legend-dot" style="background:#10b981"></div>DMT RNN (stays close)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The fix is <strong>DAgger Memory Training (DMT)</strong> — a brief fine-tuning phase that exposes the RNN to its own trajectory and trains it to autocorrect. Instead of (encoder_mt, xt₊1) → encoder_mt₊1, DMT trains on (RNN_m̂t, xt₊1) → encoder_mt₊1. The target is still the encoder's trajectory, but the input is the RNN's own (possibly drifted) state.</p>

<p>This is <em>on-policy imitation learning</em> (DAgger), as opposed to the off-policy behavior cloning in SMT. The RNN learns not just to predict the next memory state correctly — it learns to correct errors when it drifts. The equilibrium is a RNN trajectory that shadows the encoder trajectory even under its own compounding errors.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">DMT Training Objective</div>
  <span class="math-var">L_dmt</span> = E<sub>t</sub>[MSE(<span class="math-var">f</span>(m̂<sub>t</sub>, x<sub>t+1</sub>), m<sub>t+1</sub>)] &nbsp; <span class="math-comment">— on-policy: RNN input, encoder target</span><br>
  <br>
  Freeze E, D &nbsp;&nbsp; Train only <span class="math-var">f</span> &nbsp;&nbsp; Use small LR &nbsp; <span class="math-comment">— lightweight finetuning</span>
</div>

<p>DMT is sequential (it unrolls the RNN), but it's a lightweight fine-tuning stage after the parallel SMT pre-training. The bulk of the computational cost — and the credit assignment — is handled by SMT. DMT is just drift correction.</p>
</section>


