<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>SMT outperforms BPTT across the board</h2>

<p>The experiments span synthetic tasks designed to isolate specific RNN properties, and real tasks (language modeling on TinyStories, pixel sequence modeling on MNIST and Sketchy). The pixel modeling task — "Attneave's task" — is particularly challenging: the model must process an image pixel-by-pixel in raster scan order, reconstructing the stroke structure from only local pixel observations, requiring associations hundreds of timesteps apart.</p>

<h3>Synthetic task results</h3>
<p>Across five synthetic tasks (gradient stability, memory capacity, state tracking, associative recall, in-context learning), SMT→DMT outperforms BPTT in all settings. BPTT struggles even on the simplest tasks when sequences grow long. The bars below show the performance gap on the hardest settings of each task (lower loss = better):</p>

