<h3>Scaling and length generalization</h3>
<p>SMT exhibits smooth scaling along three axes: context length Tc, memory state size M, and model parameter count. Notably, the SMT-trained RNN <em>generalizes better than its Transformer teacher</em> on sequence lengths longer than training — the RNN's inductive bias (finite state machine) is better suited to extrapolation than the Transformer's (in-context lookup table).</p>

<p>Perhaps most striking is the compression scaling result: for a fixed performance target, more training compute enables the model to achieve that performance with a <em>smaller</em> memory state. Compression itself becomes a scaling axis — an unexplored direction for future sequence models.</p>
</section>

